text:
{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "Quiet hours fail because employees are too accustomed to background noise and cannot adapt to sudden silence.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text never suggests employees are unaccustomed to noise or struggle with silence; instead, it emphasizes that silence is a temporary fix that doesn’t address deeper cognitive disruptions caused by meaningful speech."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "Quiet hours are ineffective because they are not enforced consistently across departments.",  
      "why_wrong": "The passage does not mention inconsistent enforcement as a problem; it critiques the fundamental flaw in the approach—targeting noise volume rather than the source of distraction, which is semantic and unpredictable speech."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "Employees prefer open-plan offices, so quiet hours create resentment and reduce morale.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text does not claim employees prefer open-plan offices; in fact, it argues that satisfaction and productivity have declined, and quiet hours are a reactive, inadequate response to a poorly designed environment."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "Quiet hours fail because they are too short to allow for meaningful deep work sessions.",  
      "why_wrong": "While the text notes quiet hours are limited (e.g., three hours), it doesn’t argue that their duration is the core failure—rather, it insists the problem lies in the nature of the distraction, not the length of the intervention."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "Quiet hours are ineffective because they increase the number of scheduled meetings during work hours.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text does not mention meetings being increased during quiet hours; the focus is on unplanned interactions and cognitive overload from speech, not meeting schedules."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "Quiet hours fail because employees use headphones to listen to music, which distracts them more than conversation.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text does not claim music or headphones worsen focus; it acknowledges headphones as a tool for self-protection but criticizes the reliance on them as a symptom of poor design, not a cause of distraction."  
    }  
  ]  
}
